-- status  : Open
-- author  : @iris
-- created : 2026-10-30T21:21:37.386151+00:00
-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/f9cbb87e-0d1d-44df-8441-0a65ba5db1e1
-- title:
--   Lemma 10, structured form — validity of the least-squares confidence sets
-- statement:
--   Consider the structured bandit protocol with model $M^\star$, rewards in $[0,1]$, and a finite class $\mathcal F\subset(\Pi\to[0,1])$ containing $f^\star$. Let $\delta>0$ and $\beta=8\log(|\mathcal F|/\delta)$. Let $\pi^1,\dots,\pi^T$ be chosen by an arbitrary, possibly randomized, decision-making algorithm, let $\hat f^t$ be empirical minimizers and $\mathcal F^t$ the confidence sets (4.5). With probability at least $1-\delta$, both of the following hold:
--
--   1. $f^\star\in\mathcal F^t$ for all $t\in[T]$;
--   2. for all $\tau\le T$, all $f\in\mathcal F^\tau$ satisfy
--   $$
--   \sum_{t=1}^{\tau-1}\mathbb E_{\pi^t\sim p^t}\bigl[(f(\pi^t)-f^\star(\pi^t))^2\bigr]\le4\beta.
--   $$
--
--   This validates the confidence sets of generalized UCB, and it shows that every function in them has low estimation error on the history. It is the only probabilistic input of Proposition 12.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mathbb E_{\pi^t\sim p^t}$ is the conditional expectation given $\mathcal H_{t-1}$. For generalized UCB $p^t$ is a point mass, so the sum is the realized one.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: Lemma 10 stated for pairs $(x^t,\pi^t)$ and functions on them → one point $\pi^t$ of an arbitrary set $\Pi$ and an arbitrary filtration; reason: taking $\Pi=\mathcal X\times\mathcal A$ with $x^t$ known to $\mathcal H_{t-1}$ gives the contextual statement, and $\mathcal X$ a point gives the structured one
--   - generalization: two claims, each with probability at least $1-\delta$ → both claims hold on one event of probability at least $1-\delta$; reason: the joint event implies each claim, and the proof of Proposition 12 (p. 60) uses them jointly
--   - encoding: with probability at least $1-\delta$ → the outer measure of the failure set is at most $\delta$; reason: this implies the probability of the good event is at least $1-\delta$, and it needs no proof that the event is measurable
--   - encoding: $r^t\in[0,1]$ almost surely → $M^\star(\cdot\mid\pi)$ is carried by $[0,1]$ for every decision $\pi$; reason: the rewards of an arbitrary algorithm are in $[0,1]$ almost surely exactly under this condition, and it makes $f^\star$ a true mean at every decision
--   - encoding: no measurability stated → every $f\in\mathcal F$ is measurable; reason: the standing convention of the source; $\Pi$ may carry the $\sigma$-algebra generated by $\mathcal F$, so no instance is lost
-- source:
--   Foster & Rakhlin, Foundations of Reinforcement Learning and Interactive Decision Making, arXiv:2312.16730v1, Lemma 10, p. 41, (3.8)–(3.10), in the notation of (4.4)–(4.5), p. 58

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_FoundationsRL_EluderUCB_meanReward
import Definitions.Def_FoundationsRL_EluderUCB_IsStructuredBanditProcess
import Definitions.Def_FoundationsRL_EluderUCB_IsEmpiricalMinimizer
import Definitions.Def_FoundationsRL_EluderUCB_confidenceSet

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped ENNReal

namespace FoundationsRL.EluderUCB

/-- **Lemma 10, structured form.** Foster & Rakhlin, arXiv:2312.16730v1, Lemma 10, p. 41, in the
notation of §4.1.1, p. 58. Let `π¹, …, πᵀ` be chosen by an arbitrary, possibly randomized, algorithm,
and `β = 8 log(|F|/δ)`. With probability at least `1 - δ`, both `f⋆ ∈ Fᵗ` for all `t ∈ [T]` and, for
all `τ ≤ T`, all `f ∈ F^τ` satisfy `∑_{t=1}^{τ-1} E_{πᵗ∼pᵗ}[(f(πᵗ) - f⋆(πᵗ))²] ≤ 4β`.

**Formalization Note.** `E_{πᵗ∼pᵗ}[·]` is the conditional expectation given `𝓗_{t-1}`. The source
states the two claims separately, each with probability at least `1 - δ`; here they hold on one event
of that probability, which is what the proof of Proposition 12 uses. The failure probability is
bounded as an outer measure, `P {ω | ¬ good ω} ≤ δ`, which needs no measurability of the event and
implies `P {ω | good ω} ≥ 1 - δ`. The contextual form of p. 41 is the case `Act = X × A`, with the
context of round `t` known to `𝓗_{t-1}`. -/
theorem lemma_10_structured {Ω Act : Type*} {mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω} [MeasurableSpace Act]
    (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (𝓗 : Filtration ℕ mΩ)
    (M : Kernel Act ℝ) [IsMarkovKernel M] (hM : ∀ p, M p (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1)ᶜ = 0)
    (F : Set (Act → ℝ)) (hfin : F.Finite)
    (hF : ∀ f ∈ F, Measurable f ∧ ∀ p, f p ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1)
    (hstar : meanReward M ∈ F) (T : ℕ) (δ : ℝ) (hδ : 0 < δ)
    (π : ℕ → Ω → Act) (r : ℕ → Ω → ℝ) (fhat : ℕ → Ω → Act → ℝ)
    (hproc : IsStructuredBanditProcess P 𝓗 M T π r)
    (hmin : ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T,
      IsEmpiricalMinimizer F (fun i => π i ω) (fun i => r i ω) t (fhat t ω)) :
    P {ω | ¬ ((∀ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T,
          meanReward M ∈ confidenceSet F (8 * Real.log (F.ncard / δ))
            (fun i => π i ω) (fun i => r i ω) t (fhat t ω)) ∧
        ∀ τ ∈ Finset.Icc 1 T,
          ∀ f ∈ confidenceSet F (8 * Real.log (F.ncard / δ))
            (fun i => π i ω) (fun i => r i ω) τ (fhat τ ω),
            ∑ t ∈ Finset.Ico 1 τ,
              (P[fun ω' => (f (π t ω') - meanReward M (π t ω')) ^ 2 | 𝓗 (t - 1)]) ω ≤
                4 * (8 * Real.log (F.ncard / δ)))} ≤ ENNReal.ofReal δ := by sorry

end FoundationsRL.EluderUCB
